"O Prophet! Fear only Allah, and do not obey the disbelievers and the hypocrites. Follow what is revealed to you from your Lord and put your trust in Allah. Indeed, Allah is All-Knowing and All-Wise.\n\nAllah has not placed two hearts inside any man. The custom of zihar has been abolished; declaring to your wives 'You are to me like my mother's back' does not make them your mothers. Nor should you regard your adopted sons as your real sons.\n\nCall adopted children by their biological fathers' names. If you do not know who their fathers are, they are your brothers in religion and your wards. There is no sin upon you in what you do by mistake, but only in what you do with premeditation in your hearts. Allah is Ever-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\n\nThe Prophet is closer to the believers than their own selves. His command takes precedence over the command of one's own desires. The Prophet's wives are the mothers of the believers. In the Book of Allah, blood relatives are closer to one another in inheritance than the other believers and the Emigrants (Muhajirun), except that you may make a bequest to your close friends.\n\nA solemn covenant was taken from the prophets—from Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and especially from Muhammad. This was so that He might question the truthful about their truthfulness, and He has prepared a painful punishment for the disbelievers.\n\nRemember Allah's favor upon you during the Battle of the Ahzab (the Confederates). Armies marched upon you, whereupon Allah sent against them a wind and unseen hosts. The cowardice of the hypocrites, their excuses, and their breach of covenants are recounted. Ultimately, Allah repelled the disbelievers in all their rage.\n\nThere is indeed an excellent example for you in the Messenger of Allah. When the believers saw the confederate armies, they said, 'This is what Allah and His Messenger promised us; Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth.' The faithful remained true to their covenant; some have fulfilled their vow and achieved martyrdom, while others are still waiting. As for the hypocrites, Allah punished them or accepted their repentance. Allah struck terror into the hearts of the People of the Book and brought them down from their fortresses, granting their lands as an inheritance to the believers.\n\nSome of the sincere believers who remained true to the covenant they made with Allah were determined to fight until they fell as martyrs. Figures such as Hamzah, Mus'ab ibn Umayr, and Anas ibn al-Nadr fulfilled their pledge and were martyred, while others, like Uthman and Talhah, await martyrdom. Allah spared the believers from fighting and repelled their enemies. Following this, the Banu Qurayzah from among the People of the Book—who had broken their treaty and supported the Confederates against the Muslims—were confronted. By the command of Gabriel, our Prophet marched upon the Banu Qurayzah, and after a siege of twenty to twenty-five days, according to the judgment rendered by Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, the men who took part in the hostilities were executed, and the women and children were taken captive.\n\nWhen the wives of our Prophet requested an increase in their maintenance and finer garments, Allah Almighty granted them the choice between the life of this world and its adornment, or Allah, His Messenger, and the abode of the Hereafter. Beginning with Aisha, all of his wives chose Allah and His Messenger. The question arose whether this incident was a delegation of the right to divorce (tafwid al-talaq) or merely presenting them with a choice. According to the narration of Aisha, this was not deemed an act of divorce.\n\nThe Prophet's wives are not like any other women; any misconduct from them entails a double punishment in proportion to their honor and elevated status. Likewise, their obedience is rewarded twofold. Addressing them, it was commanded that they not speak in soft, flirtatious tones, lest anyone in whose heart is a sickness be moved with desire. Furthermore, they were commanded to abide settled in their homes, not to display their charms as was done in the times of former ignorance (Jahiliyyah), to establish prayer, give zakah, and obey Allah and His Messenger. These verses made obligatory upon the Prophet's wives not only the standard veil (hijab), but also complete seclusion (khidr), meaning not appearing before non-mahram men at all. The expression 'O Ahl al-Bayt (people of the household)' encompasses the Messenger of Allah's wives, children, and close household members. The Shia's restriction of Ahl al-Bayt to Ali, Fatimah, Hasan, and Husayn alone contradicts the explicit wording of the verse.\n\nAllah has prepared forgiveness and an immense reward for Muslim men and Muslim women, believing men and believing women, the devout, the truthful, the patient, the humble, the charitable, the fasting, the men and women who guard their chastity, and the men and women who remember Allah frequently. This verse was revealed following the commandments directed at the Prophet's wives, when the believing women asked, 'Will nothing be revealed concerning us?' or when the Prophet's wives expressed concern, wondering, 'Will our obedience not be accepted?'\n\nWhen Allah and His Messenger decree a matter, no believing man or believing woman has any choice remaining in their affair. This verse was revealed because our Prophet wished to arrange the marriage of his paternal aunt's daughter, Zaynab, to his freed slave, Zayd, but both initially objected. Zayd ibn Harithah had been captured in his childhood, presented as a gift to our Prophet, and was subsequently freed and adopted by him as a son. When his father and uncle arrived to take him back, Zayd chose to remain with our Prophet. Later, he was married to Zaynab, but because Zaynab looked down on him, he eventually sought a divorce. Our Prophet kept saying to him, 'Retain your wife and fear Allah,' while concealing within himself that Allah would marry Zaynab to him. When Zayd ultimately divorced her, Allah gave her in marriage to our Prophet, thereby removing any restriction for the believers regarding marrying the divorced wives of their adopted sons. This incident abolished the pagan practice of legal adoption and demonstrated that, in accordance with the established Sunnah of the prophets before him, there is no restriction upon our Prophet in that which Allah has ordained for him. Muhammad (peace be upon him) is not the father of any of your men; he is the Messenger of Allah and the Seal of the Prophets.\n\nRegarding the story of Zaynab and Zayd, it has been discussed whether this incident took place in the manner often sensationalized. According to Elmalılı, there is nothing objectionable in the occurrence of this event, as it in no way compromises the prophets' inherent sinlessness and immunity from sin (ismah). However, from the perspective of hadith scholarship, the romanticized details are not found in authentic sources. Moreover, it is rationally impossible that the Messenger of Allah was seeing Zaynab's beauty for the first time, as Zaynab was his close relative and he had known her well beforehand. In truth, Zaynab had been reluctant from the outset because she did not consider Zayd her equal, entering the marriage solely in compliance with the Messenger of Allah's command. When Zayd could no longer endure this strained relationship and wished to separate, the Prophet advised him to keep his wife and to fear Allah.\n\nThe phrasing in the verse, 'You were concealing within yourself what Allah would bring to light,' points to the fact that while the Prophet recognized the suitability of Zaynab's divorce, he hid this thought out of concern for public gossip and people's reactions. Aisha remarked that if the Prophet were ever to conceal any part of the revelation, this verse would have been the one he would have hidden. This demonstrates the Prophet's profound piety, honesty, and uncompromising truthfulness. Once Zayd dissolved the marriage and her waiting period (iddah) expired, Allah contracted the marriage between them, establishing an important legal precedent for the ummah: that it is entirely permissible to marry women divorced by one's adopted sons, because adopted sons are not biological sons.\n\nIn reply to the Christians who raised objections against the Prophet's marriage, it was declared that there is no reproach or hardship upon the Prophet in what Allah has made lawful for him. Previous prophets also married; that John (Yahya) and Jesus (Isa) did not marry was not due to a prohibition, but was rather their personal circumstance. The command of Allah is a decree definitively determined and does not exceed the capacity of those bound by religious duty. The prophets conveyed the messages of Allah and feared none besides Him. And Allah is sufficient as a Reckoner.\n\nMuhammad (peace be upon him) is not the father of any of your men. Even though he had sons, because they did not survive to adulthood, they do not fall under the category of mature men (rijal). He is the Messenger of Allah and the last of the prophets. The term 'khatam' denotes both that which concludes and that which seals. He is a divine seal attesting to the truth of all preceding prophets; it is through his prophethood that the true reality and missions of earlier prophets are definitively confirmed.\n\nThe believers are commanded to remember Allah frequently and to glorify Him morning and evening. Allah and His angels shower mercy upon the believers. The Prophet was sent as a witness, a bearer of glad tidings, a warner, a caller to Allah, and an illuminating lamp. One must not obey the disbelievers and the hypocrites, must pay no heed to their harassment, and must place trust in Allah.\n\nThe believers are informed that if they marry believing women and divorce them before touching them, no waiting period ('iddah) is required, and they must grant them a provision (mut'ah). Exclusively for the Prophet, the wives to whom he gave their bridal due (mahr), the concubines from the spoils of war, the relatives who emigrated, and believing women who offer themselves have been made lawful. Furthermore, he is free to defer whichever of his wives he wishes or take to himself whichever he wishes. This regulation is to please their hearts and bring them comfort.\n\nAfter this, it is not lawful for the Prophet to marry other women or to exchange his current wives for others; only concubines are an exception. The believers are also commanded not to enter the houses of the Prophet before the proper time, but to enter when invited for a meal and then disperse. If they ask the wives of the Prophet for something they need, they should ask from behind a screen (hijab). This is purer for their hearts. It is forbidden to cause distress to the Prophet and to marry his wives after him.\n\nIn the 53rd verse, Allah Almighty commanded the believers not to enter the Prophet's house without permission and unseasonably. Two situations are at issue here: The first is the private state when one is alone with the Prophet, in which it is obligatory not to disturb him. The second is the moment when he is in public, in which respect must be shown to him. The occasion of revelation of the verse was that during the wedding feast for the marriage with Zaynab, some individuals arrived uninvited and sat for a long time, disturbing the Prophet. This verse also commands the hijab (veiling) for the Prophet's wives and explains the meaning of their being the mothers of the believers.\n\nIn verse 54, Allah states that He knows everything, whether you disclose it or conceal it, and that nothing remains hidden from Him. In verse 55, it is emphasized that the Prophet's wives are under no obligation to veil in the presence of their fathers, their sons, their brothers, the children of their brothers and sisters, their own women, and their slaves, but that they must still fear Allah. Because paternal and maternal uncles are not explicitly mentioned among these exceptions, some scholars considered it disliked (makruh) to sit unveiled in their presence.\n\nIn verse 56, it is stated that Allah and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet, and the believers are called upon to send blessings and peace upon him as well. This shows that sending blessings (salawat) upon the Prophet is obligatory (fard). In the hadiths, it is stated that whoever does not send blessings when the Prophet's name is mentioned is reproached. In verses 57–58, it is stated that those who abuse Allah and His Messenger are cursed in this world and the hereafter, and those who harm believing men and believing women for what they have not done have burdened themselves with slander and a manifest sin.\n\nIn verse 59, the Prophet's wives, his daughters, and all believing women are commanded to draw their cloaks (jilbabs) closely around themselves. This is so that they may be recognized and not harassed. The jilbab refers to garments that cover from head to toe, such as the chador and ferace (loose cloak). This verse reinforces the modest covering (hijab) of free women and aims to protect their chastity and dignity. In verses 60–62, the hypocrites, those in whose hearts is a disease, and those who spread false rumors in the city are warned that if they do not desist, the Prophet will be incited against them, and they will be cursed, seized, and slain.\n\nVerses 63–68 recount that knowledge of the Hour of Resurrection is with Allah alone, that a blazing fire has been prepared for the disbelievers, and that they will say in regret, \"If only we had obeyed Allah and obeyed the Messenger.\" In verses 69–71, the believers are commanded not to be like those who abused Moses (Musa), to fear Allah, and to speak words of appropriate justice and soundness. In verses 72–73, the concept of the Trust (amanah / moral responsibility) is explained: Allah offered the Trust to the heavens, the earth, and the mountains, but they refused to bear it, while humankind undertook this heavy responsibility. Humankind is indeed very unjust and ignorant; yet Allah, who accepts the repentance of the believers, is Forgiving and Merciful.\n\nThe wisdom of the earth and the outcome of undertaking the Trust is this: Allah will certainly punish the hypocrite men and hypocrite women, and the polytheist men and polytheist women, requiting them for their betrayal of the Trust and their wrongdoing.\n\nAs for the believing men and believing women—that is, the believers who strive to fulfill the Trust—Allah will accept their repentance and show them His Divine Countenance through His mercy and forgiveness. Allah is Forgiving and deeply Merciful.\n\nIf the hypocrites and polytheists also repent and embrace faith, Allah will accept their repentance and forgive them as well. That is to say, just as the end of those who do not fulfill the Trust is terrible, those who obey Allah and His Messenger and fulfill the Trust with faith attain the greatest triumph and achieve the Divine Countenance.\n\nThis blessing and mercy are worthy of praise and gratitude. For this reason, how fitting it is that Surah Saba, which follows immediately after, begins with \"praise to Allah\": \"Praise be to Allah, the Lord of the worlds.\"\n\nThis, then, is the outcome of the Trust: those who betray it meet with punishment, while those who remain faithful attain Allah's mercy and His Divine Countenance. This is a manifestation of Allah's justice and mercy."